    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "How to read a failure here",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 7,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 32
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false
        },
        "content": "**Work down this list before concluding the site is down.**\n\n| What you see | What it usually means |\n|---|---|\n| Connection-level failures high, HTTP-error failures 0 | Nothing reached the server. A firewall is dropping this machine (rate limit, fail2ban, CSF), or the host is unreachable. **The site may be perfectly fine for everyone else.** |\n| HTTP-error failures high | The server replied and refused. Its own problem: 500, 403, a WAF rule. Check the site's error log. |\n| 'Getting connected' drops to 0 | The connection never completed. Same as the first row. |\n| Slowest response pinned at exactly 20s | Probes are being cut off by the scrape timeout, so the true time is *at least* that. Not a measurement, a floor. |\n| Median low, 95th percentile high | Intermittent, not structurally slow. Look for cron jobs, backups, a noisy neighbour on shared hosting. |\n| Transfer dominates 'Where the time goes' | Page weight, not server speed. Check compression and image sizes. |\n\n**This stack only watches while this machine is awake**, so blank stretches in the timeline are gaps in observation, not proven uptime. See `docs/Decisions.md` D-014.\n\n**Before escalating:** load the site from a phone on mobile data. If it works there and not here, the problem is between this machine and the server."
      }
    }
